    "knowledge_point": "Solving Quadratic “Or Equal To” Inequalities That Require Rearrangement",
    "problem": "Find the solution of $x+3\\ge 2{x}^{2}.$",
    "worked_solution": "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the numbers to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & \\ge 2{x}^{2} \\\\ -2{x}^{2}+x+3 & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=-2{x}^{2}+x+3$ lies **above** the $x$-axis or touches this axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}+x+3 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-x-3 & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-3x)+(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x(2x-3)+(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+1)(x-\\frac{3}{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-1$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029391/assets/86d7fbea75961bdb83c014ca9febb735e6a1745f71e8e094de416998b2072630.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}+x+3\\ge 0,\n$$\n\n \nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **above** the $x$-axis or touches this axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $-1\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in [-1,\\frac{3}{2}].$",
